Apple's latest iPhone upgrade cycle is showing stronger-than-expected demand, with buyers favoring more expensive models and storage configurations, according to an Evercore ISI consumer survey seen by <a href="https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/survey-shows-apple-is-seeing-a-betterthanexpected-iphone-refresh-cycle-4907449"><em>Investing.com</em></a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-18-pro-max.jpg" alt="" width="1672" height="941" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1066063" /><br/>
Evercore surveyed nearly 4,000 consumers in the United States. Among respondents, 67% identified their existing iPhone's age as the main reason to upgrade, a substantial increase from 48% in the previous year's survey.<br/>
<br/>
The purchase preferences also suggest that buyers remain willing to spend more on Apple's higher-end devices. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> and Pro Max together attracted 53% of prospective buyers, with 32% choosing the Pro Max, compared with 29% a year earlier.<br/>
<br/>
Evercore estimates that average iPhone selling prices could increase by approximately 28%. Its forecast combines higher retail prices with a greater proportion of purchases going toward premium models and larger storage configurations, including the reported 2TB options for the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; models and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The foldable &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; attracted interest from 14% of respondents. Evercore believes that figure could rise as its mid-October launch approaches, although the survey currently places it well behind the combined Pro models.<br/>
<br/>
Interest extended to other Apple products, with 38% of respondents planning an Apple Watch purchase and 43% considering AirPods. Both figures increased from the previous year.<br/>
<br/>
